{"author":"Rebar Bot Nava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nThe lexical tier reaches semantic recall@5 of 0.823. ADR 0122 measured one candidate that beats it distinguishably at k=5: `vector` — a digest of each ticket, embedded and ranked by cosine similarity — at 0.876, McNemar p=0.0213, recovering 13 pairs and losing 3. That single comparison is the whole justification for this tier, and it is worth 93 otherwise-invisible duplicate pairs.\n\nrebar runs against diverse model providers, so this tier must not be reachable through one vendor only, nor become a dependency the feature cannot survive: an installation with no provider still needs similar-ticket surfacing to work.\n\n## Approach\n\n**Portability is delivered by the lexical tier, not by this one.** The index sibling ships retrieval and the surfacing sibling the advisory, with no provider, no credentials and no network between them — that is what makes similar-ticket surfacing available in every installation. This tier is an opt-in ranking upgrade that requires *some* embedding provider and degrades to lexical whenever one is absent or failing. Saying so is what keeps the portability claim true: a tier that needs a provider cannot honestly be called credential-free.\n\n**Vendor-neutrality comes from a seam rebar's runtime already ships, and the install layer is part of the opt-in.** The installed distribution is `pydantic-ai-slim` 1.107.9, already the LLM runtime behind `src/rebar/llm/providers.py`. It exposes a first-class embeddings API — `Embedder`, `EmbeddingModel`, `EmbeddingResult`, `EmbeddingSettings`, `infer_embedding_model` and `KnownEmbeddingModelName`, each verified present on `pydantic_ai.embeddings`, plus `OpenAIEmbeddingsCompatibleProvider` (`pydantic_ai/embeddings/__init__.py:78`) for OpenAI-compatible endpoints. Dispatching a `provider:model` string through `infer_embedding_model` is the shape `providers.py` already uses for chat models, so this tier is a configured model name over an existing seam.\n\n`pydantic-ai-slim` is not among rebar's core `dependencies` (`pyyaml`, `jsonschema`, `referencing`); it arrives through the `[agents]` extra, and the Bedrock default needs the separate `[bedrock]` extra. Measured in a dev install, `pydantic_ai.embeddings`, `.bedrock`, `.openai` and `.test` import, while `.cohere`, `.google`, `.voyageai` and `.sentence_transformers` each raise `ImportError` naming their own optional group. A missing backend is therefore an ordinary expected state, not a broken install, and must degrade exactly like a provider failure. rebar takes none of these dependencies, and no offline embedding path is claimed.\n\n**Both model choices are configuration, and neither pins a vendor.** The embedding model defaults to `bedrock:amazon.titan-embed-text-v2:0`, 1,024 dimensions, cosine; the digest model to `bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-haiku-4-5-20251001-v1:0`, resolved through the same provider-qualified seam rather than constructed directly. Both are the models ADR 0122 measured, named so the result is reproducible, and both are defaults an operator overrides with a model string.\n\n**Digest step.** The 4-field prompt pinned at `f147e08242f`, input capped at 40,000 characters, per ADR 0122's architecture section. The digest is the weakest link and the plan says so: `vector` is *not* distinguishable from `vector-raw`, which skips it (p=0.0654, p=0.0625), and survives only because `vector-raw` also fails to beat `fts5` (p=0.6636) — a non-transitive chain ADR 0122 states plainly. Neither dropping it nor claiming it helps is supported by current evidence.\n\n**Two counters ADR 0122 makes mandatory for any consumer.** Its Consequences section records that the digest parser silently returned empty fields for three reply shapes, each indistinguishable from success without an `empties` counter, and that 2 of 630 tickets did not answer the task, both succeeding on retry — making retry correct handling, not a workaround. Omitting either reproduces a diagnosed defect.\n\n**Ranking swaps behind an interface that already works.** The index, its freshness map and read-phase catch-up come from the index sibling; the four-surface advisory from the surfacing sibling. This tier replaces only the scoring function and adds the digest-and-embed build step. k stays at 5: ADR 0122's decision is scoped there, and at k=10 `vector`'s edge is indistinguishable (p=0.3438).\n\n**`TestEmbeddingModel` is a test double, not evidence of portability.** Verified importable at `pydantic_ai.embeddings.test`, it makes ranking deterministic offline and carries behaviour tests only; the second-provider claim rests on resolution through `infer_embedding_model`.\n\nAny ratcheted mechanism this tier adds — the two model config keys are the expected ones — carries a `# mechanism-ok:` marker at its definition site naming this ticket, since the mechanism-delta ratchet fails on any unadmitted new mechanism.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] An embedding model configured as a `provider:model` string is resolved through `infer_embedding_model`, and retrieval ranks by cosine similarity over the resulting vectors. Checked by: a test configuring `TestEmbeddingModel` and asserting the ordering follows cosine similarity rather than the lexical score.\n- [ ] Configuring a model string for at least two distinct providers resolves to each provider's embedding model with no code change and no vendor branch in rebar. Checked by: a test parameterized over `bedrock:` and `openai:` model strings asserting `infer_embedding_model` returns the matching model class for each, both of which import in a dev install so neither case is skipped.\n- [ ] The digest model is resolved from a configured provider-qualified model string rather than a hard-coded vendor model. Checked by: a test configuring a non-Bedrock digest model string and asserting the resolved model matches it.\n- [ ] With no embedding model configured, retrieval returns the lexical tier's results and the create exits 0. Checked by: a test asserting the advisory matches the lexical tier's output when the key is unset.\n- [ ] When the configured backend's optional dependency is not installed, retrieval falls back to lexical-only with a diagnostic naming the missing group, and the create exits 0. Checked by: a test configuring a model string whose backend raises `ImportError`, asserting exit status, advisory contents and the diagnostic.\n- [ ] When the configured provider raises a denial, retrieval falls back to lexical-only with a diagnostic and the create exits 0. Checked by: a test asserting exit status, advisory contents and the diagnostic.\n- [ ] When the configured provider times out, retrieval falls back to lexical-only with a diagnostic and the create exits 0. Checked by: a test asserting exit status, advisory contents and the diagnostic.\n- [ ] When the digest reply parses to empty fields, retrieval falls back to lexical-only with a diagnostic and the create exits 0. Checked by: a test asserting exit status, advisory contents and the diagnostic.\n- [ ] An `empties` counter records every digest reply that parses to an empty field and is observable after an index build. Checked by: a test feeding each of the three reply shapes ADR 0122 records, asserting the counter equals 3 while the build still completes.\n- [ ] A digest reply that does not answer the task is retried, and a success on retry is recorded as a success. Checked by: a test whose model fails once then succeeds, asserting the ticket is indexed and the retry is counted.\n- [ ] rebar's declared dependencies are unchanged by this ticket. Checked by: `pyproject.toml`'s dependency and optional-dependency lists are untouched in the diff.\n- [ ] Every module added or modified under `src/rebar/` is at most 800 lines. Checked by: the module-size gate.\n- [ ] `check_mechanism_delta.py --check` exits 0, with any new mechanism admitted by a `# mechanism-ok:` marker naming this ticket. Checked by: running it.\n- [ ] `check_complexity_baseline.py --check` exits 0. Checked by: running it."}},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1793599174759699001,"uuid":"8248ccf4-f2f9-404f-bc90-aa110b1c5eef"}